Good, generated map view also ignores the second parameter. So the legacy and generated routes are preserved.

Now let me think about an independent mutation. Actually, I think the controls are sufficient. Let me document my finding and move on.

Actually, let me think more carefully. The brief says I should do ONE independent mutation. Let me try a meaningful one.

Looking at the source change in referenceBattleGame.ts:
```typescript
private clickProvince(id: string, inspect = false): void {
    if (this.disposed) return;
    const { state, from, to, mode, running, paused } = this.controller.view;
    if (this.computerActive() || running || paused || state.pendingCapture || state.phase === "finished" || state.phase === "human-loss") return;
```

What if I mutated to remove JUST the `computerActive()` check from the new guard? The BattleController has `accepts()` which checks `allowInput`. During computer turn in automatic mode, `allowInput` returns false (active player is computer), so accepts returns false. So select() returns false. So no state change.

But there's a subtle thing: the BattleController might still record a "select attempt" even if it returns false. Let me check...

Actually, looking at select():
```
select(from: string | null, to: string | null, revision?: number): boolean {
    if (!this.accepts(revision) || this.current.running || this.current.paused || this.pending() || this.state.phase !== "attack") return false;
    if (this.current.mode === "move") {
      ...
    } else {
      if (from === null ? to !== null : !province(this.state.map, from) || ownerOf(this.state, from) !== activePlayer(this.state)?.id) return false;
      if (to !== null && (from === null || whyNot(this.state, from, to) !== "")) return false;
    }
    this.cancel();
    const changed = from !== this.current.from || to !== this.current.to;
    this.publish({ from, to, effectiveThreshold: this.clamp(from), message: "",
      ...(changed ? { lastOutcome: null, rolls: [], ties: 0, attackerWins: 0, defenderWins: 0 } : {}),
    });
    return true;
}
```

If accepts() returns false, select returns false WITHOUT calling publish. So no state change. 

But wait, I just realized the test does check the state via save(page). If clicks during computer turn don't change state (because BattleController blocks), then test passes. So removing computerActive from clickProvince doesn't break.

Let me try one more mutation. Let me look at the BattleController's `select` method:
```
if (to !== null && (from === null || whyNot(this.state, from, to) !== "")) return false;
```

Hmm. Let me look at why select returns false:
- accepts() fails (computer turn etc.)
- current.running (continuous mode)
- current.paused
- pending() (capture)
- state.phase !== "attack"
- move mode checks
- battle mode: from invalid OR from not owned OR to invalid (whyNot)

So if I removed the `whyNot` check from select, then even invalid attacks could be selected. Then clickProvince would call singleExchange. singleExchange calls canExchange which checks whyNot. So singleExchange returns false. The exchange wouldn't happen.

Wait, what if the select call succeeds (no whyNot check), and singleExchange is called. canExchange checks `whyNot(this.state, from, to) === ""`. So if whyNot is non-empty, canExchange returns false. singleExchange returns false. No exchange happens.

So removing whyNot check in select doesn't break anything.

OK, I've exhausted my ideas. Let me just confirm my analysis with an additional independent mutation. Let me try removing the BattleController's `this.pending()` check in select.

Actually wait, the BattleController's changes are NOT in this PR. They're in src/ui/battleController.ts which is unchanged. So mutating it would be testing pre-existing code.

